00:00:00.030 --> 00:00:02.060 align:start position:0%
 
well as we were just discussing there  this week the government announced an  unprecedented incentive measures to  support the economy through coronavirus  they are prepared to step up and pay up  to 80 percent of people's wages if they  would lose their jobs otherwise and also  provide billions of pounds of loans and  grants to businesses well the CBI worked  with the government and trade unions to  come up with this package under director  general Carolyn Fairburn joins us now  from Winchester thank you for being on  the program today you what would be good  to come up with these measures are you  happy with the final package well it was  an extraordinary package at the end of  the day I mean last week was it's  fundamentally a human crisis but last  week it really became an economic crisis  as thousands if not millions of jobs  were on the line and I had employers  phoning me in tears at the prospect of  having to let so many people go and the  package that was put in place on Friday  recognized two things fundamentally that  this was about keeping people in jobs so  they have livelihood and that we have an  economy that we can build when we come  out of this and also cash so the loans  and indeed the the foregoing of  suspension of tax so unprecedented  package there will need to be more and I  know the government is working on that  now but it was a breakthrough yeah you  say that there will need to be more man  we heard from Jamie Corbin earlier who  was talking about self-employed people  ninety four pounds a week not enough to  live on  talking about people who are renting in  that situation in particular what do you  think the government does need to do  well 94 pounds it is not enough for the  self-employed to live on and I know that  the government with with business and  with the Federation of small business  are working as we speak to find a  solution to this I think we just have to  recognize that this is totally  unprecedented what we're facing and the  pace at which we can do this is  determined by the people working on it  flat out coming up with solutions across  business in government that is a  priority another priority is that there  are some businesses in that mid size  range between small and large who are  also going to need a funding that's a  gap at the moment that's being worked on  and Sophia the final thing that has to  happen is that the banks ensuring that  the money is getting out of the door and  we're seeing a lot of action on that as  we speak as well as you say is  unprecedented I mean the amount of sums  that we could be talking about over a  prolonged period of time is mind-blowing  the sort of money that we'll all be  paying off perhaps a whole lifetimes is  it possible for the government to be  able to arrange this kind of money well  I think it is because we don't have a  this was not an economic problem when it  started so we have a fundamentally  strong economy if we can protect it if  we can keep people in jobs if we can  protect our businesses then when this  virus passes we will be able to rebuild  so it is a task worth borrowing for is a  task I think worth fighting for and the  way that we saw last week I think  business and government and unions and  communities come together is the way  forward it felt like the beginning of  the fight back Sophie it will take big  borrowing but if we can have a strong  economy coming out of the other end it  would have been worth it I mean what  we'll also need is for businesses to do  their part to act responsibly to not lay  people off to not take advantage of any  loopholes in this very hastily drawn up  legislation are you confident that  businesses will act responsibly I am  really confident of that what we are  seeing across the country is an  extraordinary stepping up by business  I'm doggin two distilleries who are now  making hand sanitizers two manufacturers  who are now moving into making  ventilators this is a uk-wide  effort that's bringing businesses and  communities together I am confident of  that if we have government creating the  incentives and the environment for them  to be able to do the right thing we will  see that so many more people are in jobs  today than were on Friday because of  action the government has taken an  action that business has taken you say  you're confident the business will act  responsibly and at the same time though  the Times reported this week that  easyJet had going to go ahead with a  hundred and seventy four million  and dividend payout to its shareholders  while at the same time asking taxpayers  for support for coronavirus I mean how  is that acceptable in any way yes yes I  think you will have a lot of businesses  who are now looking to how they control  their costs and how they protect their  people and you and I do urge businesses  to think very carefully about how they  make those judgments the only point I  was anything wrong is that we are all  [ __ ] sorry was the easy chat wrong I'm  not going to make comments I'm not going  to make comments on particular companies  I know that companies are looking  extremely hard of how they make this  fair this can't be made right for  everybody it's a no government can make  this right for everybody and no business  can make it right for everybody but I do  know that businesses are looking at how  they make this fair and we do need to  remember that we are all shareholders  through our pensions through our savings  we are all shareholders so how that is  it's done and I do work businesses too  to look very hard at how we make the  sacrifice fair across our country and I  think that's what the package on Friday  has helped us to do and businesses will  support every way they possibly can and  just finally I just want to take a step  back and just really take in just how  unprecedented this really is I mean a UK  government for the first time saying  they'll step in to pay people's wages  we've never seen that in wartime we've  never seen that in any previous  recessions and our economics editor  Eddie Conway described it as capitalism  being paused do you think that things  will ever be the same again I do think  that we are going to look we are going  to change as a result of this we are  going to be allocating our capital our  people in different ways to make sure  that this doesn't happen again in the  same way to prepare for pandemics to  have track and tracing systems that  enable us to to do things differently so  yes yes I do but on the other hand I do  think that markets are fantastically  good at delivering innovation and  getting people and capital to the right  things we are beginning to see that  we're beginning to see resources reused  across the economy to make ventilators  to do  the right thing and I think we will see  more of that so markets I think have a  lot to bring to this although there will  be lessons that we reflect on I'm sure  of that  okay Carolyn Fairburn thank you very  much for being on the program today  thank you thank you thank you

